SCOPE OF THE LICENSE GRANT

The Apache-2.0 grant above covers all original material under Slopesmith/, except where a
sub-path says otherwise below. That is the rule; the following are examples of it rather than
the boundary: the editor and API service under src/, the checks under scripts/, the offline
tooling under tools/, the Blender bridge under blender/, the prop models under
tools/prop-recipes/props/, and the accompanying prose in README.md and docs/.

One scoped exception: tools/retopology/quadwild-patches/ holds modifications to QuadWild-BiMDF,
quadretopology, and QuadriFlow. Those patch files are derivative works conveyed under their
respective upstream licenses; see tools/retopology/quadwild-patches/README.md and the license files beside it.
Nothing else in this repository is affected — Slopesmith invokes the resulting binaries as separate
processes and links no part of them.

The grant does NOT extend to, and this repository does not redistribute:

  * SSX Tricky, its executables, disc contents, level data, textures, audio, or any other retail
    game asset.
  * Any trademark of Electronic Arts Inc.

Retail files and assets are generated locally by the user from their own copy of the game. The editor
reads them from a Maps folder and a workspace folder configured under File ▸ Settings, and both are
excluded from version control. Machine-readable executable routing and map-geometry metadata needed by the editor are
generated into that local Maps folder by Snowknife; they are not included in Slopesmith's source or
distribution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RETAIL-DERIVED FACTS

A bounded class of retail-derived facts is carried in the source itself: format and asset
identifiers, enumeration, sprite and bank names, and measured functional constants. The authoritative data copies are
Trailmap/specs/data/external-sound-banks-v1.json and Trailmap/specs/data/ride-v1.json, plus the per-course race timing
constants (lap counts and showoff clock lengths read from the executable's own tables) in src/core/doc/race.ts. These
are the functional interface the editor interoperates through, retained because interoperating requires them. Beyond
that interface, individual measured values appear in tests and design notes as the evidence a behaviour was checked
against -- a record's field values, the node counts of a music graph, a texel colour, the measured layout of a retail
course -- each cited to the Trailmap chapter or the local extraction that established it. None of this is accompanied
by any retail code, asset, or executable; everything else is generated locally from your own copy of the game and
stays out of version control.

